UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 2, Lesson 4: Handwriting speed and fluency', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Handwriting fluency means keeping letter shape and spacing readable at a comfortable pace, then rewriting only unclear words rather than racing.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nList three checks you will use before speeding up a copy of a 40-word paragraph.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLetter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape.\n\nCHECK\nPressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCopy a 40–60 word paragraph twice (careful, then comfortable). Time the second copy, circle unclear letters, rewrite two words and note one way you kept your hand comfortable.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Handwriting fluency means keeping letter shape and spacing readable at a comfortable pace, then rewriting only unclear words rather than racing.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Copy a 40–60 word paragraph twice (careful, then comfortable). Time the second copy, circle unclear letters, rewrite two words and note one way you kept your hand comfortable. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape. Reasoning: I used legibility by attending to fluency rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes. Correction: Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape. Why: the correction restores accurate use of legibility.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating legibility on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with legibility, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape. This matches the lesson skill for legibility.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape.","Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes."],"question":"List three checks you will use before speeding up a copy of a 40-word paragraph. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes.","Handwriting fluency means keeping letter shape and spacing readable at a comfortable pace, then rewriting only unclear words rather than racing.","Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape.","Careful use of fluency with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising legibility?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named fluency, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the fluency used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"A learner is practising legibility. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries legibility.","options":["Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show legibility and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words."],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses legibility from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about legibility?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise fluency?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about legibility.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of legibility to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating legibility on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape. Reasoning: I used legibility by attending to fluency rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with legibility, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes. Correction: Letter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape. Why: the correction restores accurate use of legibility.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w02-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise legibility.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use legibility with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify fluency and explain why it matters.","I can practise spacing independently on a new prompt.","I can notice pace needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Handwriting fluency means keeping letter shape and spacing readable at a comfortable pace, then rewriting only unclear words rather than racing. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Handwriting fluency means keeping letter shape and spacing readable at a comfortable pace, then rewriting only unclear words rather than racing.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about legibility?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCopy 1 (careful): even height, finger-spaces. Copy 2 (comfortable pace): still readable. Circle two unclear letters in copy 2 and rewrite those words only.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList three checks you will use before speeding up a copy of a 40-word paragraph.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLetter size steadiness; word spaces; distinct look-alikes (a/o, r/n). Speed comes after shape.","explain":"Handwriting fluency means keeping letter shape and spacing readable at a comfortable pace, then rewriting only unclear words rather than racing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Pressing harder or writing larger does not fix crushed letter shapes. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Careful then fluent handwriting copies.","caption":"Shape before speed.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Careful copy</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">even height + spaces</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Comfortable pace</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">still readable</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Copy 1 (careful): even height, finger-spaces. Copy 2 (comfortable pace): still readable. Circle two unclear letters in c Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Copy 1 (careful): even height, finger-spaces. Copy 2 (comfortable pace): still readable. Circle two unclear letters in c Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about legibility?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCopy 1 (careful): even height, finger-spaces. Copy 2 (comfortable pace): still readable. Circle two unclear letters in copy 2 and rewrite those words only. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice